<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ I</p>
      <h2 id="principle">The principle</h2>
    </div>
    <p>An org team runs one feature as a flat pod of peer sessions. Each peer owns a lane of the work and decides within it; the forks it cannot settle alone travel up to the lead, and the ones only a human can answer travel up to you.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Swarm mode parallelizes the components of one spec inside a single session. Org mode parallelizes the lanes of one spec across several full sessions. A feature that splits into independent lanes finishes in roughly the time of its slowest lane instead of the sum of all of them.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ II</p>
      <h2 id="peer">Peers, not subagents</h2>
    </div>
    <p>A peer is a full Claude Code session, with its own skills, its own subagents, and its own judgment. That is what lets a peer decide. A subagent executes a pre-decided recipe; a peer reasons in its own main context.</p>
  </div>
  <p>An org team is a flat pod of up to four peers connected to the MCP broker pool. They are flat for claiming work: any free peer claims any unblocked lane. The lead can also direct a lane at a named peer when it wants to control allocation, and one of the four wears the lead hat, the relay between the pod and you.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ III</p>
      <h2 id="decide">Decide in-lane, escalate out</h2>
    </div>
    <p>A peer decides its own in-lane implementation choices: how to structure a function, which existing helper to reuse, where a boundary sits. Those are the peer's to make, in its own main context.</p>
  </div>
  <p>A fork it cannot settle within its lane, it escalates rather than guesses. A cross-lane choice (one whose answer changes another lane) or an un-decidable one (one that needs design or product judgment) is not the peer's call. It raises the fork instead of deciding for the team.</p>

  <h3 id="channel">The escalation channel</h3>
  <p>Two paths carry a fork upward. A fork tied to a specific task uses <code>yield_fork</code>. A free-form question, one that is not bound to a single task, uses <code>ask_lead</code>; the broker delivers it to the lead, who replies with <code>answer_peer</code>. When a fork needs human judgment, the lead brings it to you and routes your answer back down. The chain is always peer to lead to human, and a message that cannot be delivered surfaces as an error rather than disappearing.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ IV</p>
      <h2 id="pipeline">The pipeline</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Org mode is the selectable <code>org</code> track. It runs the standard spec-driven workflow with one substitution at the implementation phase: <code>org-dispatch</code> replaces solo <code>/tdd</code>.</p>
  </div>
  <p><code>org-dispatch</code> reads the approved spec, splits it into lane-tagged tasks, and hands them to the pod over the broker. Peers claim a lane, build it, and signal done so dependent lanes unblock; the lead commits between rounds so a dependent round starts from finished work. Everything around that phase, intake through approval and cleanup through commit, is the same pipeline every other track uses.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ V</p>
      <h2 id="when">When to run an org team</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Reach for org mode when a feature splits into lanes several sessions could own at once, and the cost you want to cut is the wait, the time a multi-part change spends queued behind one decision-maker.</p>
  </div>
  <p>For a single-session change, stay on solo <code>/tdd</code>. For parallel components inside one session, use swarm. An org team earns its coordination only when the lanes are genuinely independent and there are real sessions to work them; below that, the coordination is overhead.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ VI</p>
      <h2 id="enable">Turning it on</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Org mode is experimental and opt-in. It is off by default and stays out of the way until you ask for it.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Enable it with <code>velocity.org_mode.enabled</code> in <code>project.json</code>; it requires a git repository. With the flag off, the <code>org</code> track is not offered and the default solo and swarm workflows are unchanged. <code>org-dispatch</code> refuses to run when the flag is off or the project is not a git work tree, so the fence is enforced rather than merely documented.</p>
  <p class="hero-readmore">
    <a data-cta="org-setup" href="{{ '/org/setup/' | rel }}"><span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span> getting started: stand up a pod and run it end to end</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ VII</p>
      <h2 id="gates">Gates stay structural</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Peers deciding in their lanes changes nothing about consent. The workflow's gates, <code>/approve-spec</code>, <code>/approve-swarm</code>, and <code>/grant-commit</code>, stay structural and cannot be satisfied by any peer or by the lead. You remain the final authority: the pod brings a decision to you, it never makes one in your place.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span>
    <div class="body">
      <strong>One feature, governed end to end.</strong>
      <p>Org mode is governed by Article X of the baseline constitution, which covers how multiple sessions coordinate. The rules for how a single session works are unchanged, so a peer is as bounded inside its own session as any solo run.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
